import <wdoc.wcl>

fact diagrams {
  title = "diagram"
  related = [{ id: "flowcharts", why: "flowchart shapes supports diagram: Flowchart shapes plus the layered auto-layout mode turn a `diagram` into a flowchart: declare the shapes and the edges, and the renderer ranks them topologically and elbow-routes the connections." }, { id: "charts", why: "charts supports diagram: wdoc ships three chart kinds — `bar_chart`, `line_chart`, and `pie_chart` — that lower to SVG via pure-WCL geometry." }]
  audience = :both
  body {
    p <<DOC
      A `diagram` is wdoc's drawing surface. It declares a `width` / `height` and holds shapes that
      break down into primitives and render as SVG. Connect two shapes by id with an `a -> b` edge;
      set `pan_zoom = true` for an interactive viewport; set a `layout` (`:layered` / `:radial` /
      `:force`) to place shapes automatically (see [layout modes](fact_layout_modes)). Higher-level
      families (flowcharts, charts, trees, maps, tilemaps, wireframes) are shapes that live inside a
      `diagram` too. The primitive shapes are catalogued below; see also the
      [connections](concept_connections) concept.
    DOC
    demo {
      diagram = true
      diagram {
        width = 320
        height = 160
        pan_zoom = true
        zoom_min = 0.5
        zoom_max = 4.0
        rect {
          id = a
          x = 20.0
          y = 30.0
          width = 80.0
          height = 50.0
          fill = "#88c0d0"
        }
        rect {
          id = b
          x = 210.0
          y = 90.0
          width = 80.0
          height = 50.0
          fill = "#a3be8c"
        }
        a -> b
      }
    }
    type_table {
      type = Diagram
    }

    h2 "Shapes"
    p "A diagram holds shapes that break down into primitives. They split across three pages:"
    list {
      li "[Primitive shapes](fact_primitive_shapes) — `rect`, `circle`, `line`, `label`, `polygon`: the base figures everything else lowers to."
      li "[Composite shapes](fact_composite_shapes) — `container`, `card`, `node_table`: shapes that hold or lay out content."
      li "[Styling shapes with classes](fact_shape_styling) — paint shapes with theme-aware `class`es instead of baked-in `fill`s."
    }
    p <<DOC
      Higher-level families (flowcharts, charts, trees, maps, tilemaps, wireframes) are shapes that
      live inside a `diagram` too; wiring is covered by the
      [connections](concept_connections) concept and placement by the [layout modes](fact_layout_modes).
    DOC
  }
}
